'''Morys George Lyndhurst Bruce, 4th Baron Aberdare''' (16 June 1919<ref>{{cite book |title=Who's Who 1998 : an Annual Biographical Dictionary |year=1998 |publisher=St. Martin's Press |location=New York |isbn=0-312-17591-4 |pages=2–3 |chapter=Aberdare, Morys George Lyndhurst Bruce}}</ref> – 23 January 2005<ref>{{Cite news |title=Obituary: Lord Aberdare |url=https://www.theguardian.com/news/2005/feb/19/guardianobituaries.politics |website=The Guardian |date=19 February 2005 |access-date=4 January 2020}}</ref>), was a [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] politician, and from 1999 until his death, one of [[List of excepted hereditary peers|ninety-two elected hereditary peers]] in the [[United Kingdom|British]] [[House of Lords]]. He was the eldest son of [[Clarence Bruce, 3rd Baron Aberdare]], and Margaret Bethune Black, and succeeded to his father's title on the latter's death in 1957.

==Education==
Bruce was educated at [[Sandroyd School]] before heading to [[Winchester College]] and [[New College, Oxford]], where he read Politics, Philosophy and Economics.

==Career==
[[File:Coats of Arms of Morys Bruce (4e baron Aberdare).svg|thumb|left|Coats of Arms of Morys Bruce]]
In 1939 he joined the [[British Army]], commissioned with the rank of [[lieutenant]] in the [[Welsh Guards]]; he would eventually reach the rank of [[Captain (British Army and Royal Marines)|captain]], after having served in various staff positions with [[XII Corps (United Kingdom)|XII Corps]], the [[21st Army Group]], and [[XXX Corps (United Kingdom)|XXX Corps]] during and after [[World War II]].

He joined the [[J. Arthur Rank]] Organisation in 1947, working there for two years before moving to the [[British Broadcasting Corporation]], where he worked between 1949 and 1956. In 1970, he became [[Minister of State]] for the [[Department of Health and Social Security]]; in 1974, he was appointed to the [[Privy Council of the United Kingdom|Privy Council]] and became a [[Minister without portfolio (United Kingdom)|Minister without Portfolio]]. Between 1976 and 1992, he served as [[Chairman of Committees (House of Lords)|Chairman of Committees]] of the House of Lords, (Deputy [[Speaker of the House of Lords]]). In 1984, he was created a [[Order of the British Empire|Knight Commander of the Order of the British Empire]], and he would serve various positions within the [[Venerable Order of Saint John|Order of St John of Jerusalem]]. After the [[House of Lords Act 1999]] prevented hereditary peers from sitting in the Lords solely by virtue of their peerages, Lord Aberdare became one of the [[List of excepted hereditary peers|ninety-two hereditary peers]] elected to stay in the House of Lords.

On 24 August 1992, he officially opened [[Chester City F.C.|Chester City]]'s new football stadium, the [[Deva Stadium]].<ref>[http://www.fsf.org.uk/ground-guide/stadiums/chester/saunders-honda-stadiumde/ The Football Supporters' Federation – Chester<!-- Bot generated title -->] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101230175455/http://www.fsf.org.uk/ground-guide/stadiums/chester/saunders-honda-stadiumde/ |date=30 December 2010 }}</ref>

Lord Aberdare was a lifelong devotee of [[real tennis]], winning the British amateur singles championship four times between 1953 and 1957, and the amateur doubles championship four times between 1954 and 1961. He served as president of the [[Tennis and Rackets Association]] from 1972 until 2004. During his tenure there was a significant expansion in both [[real tennis]] and [[Racquets (sport)|rackets]], and a number of new courts were built while several others were re-opened. His book, ''The JT Faber Book of Tennis and Rackets'' (London: Quiller Press, 2001. {{ISBN|1-899163-62-X}}), is the most comprehensive modern reference for these sports.

Lord Aberdare was President of the London Welsh Trust, which runs the [[London Welsh Centre]], from 1959 until 1962, and from 1969 to 1970.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.londonwelsh.org/archives/1796 |title=Our Former Presidents: London Welsh Centre |publisher=[[London Welsh Centre]] |year=2010 |access-date=4 February 2011 |work=[[London Welsh Centre]] website |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110720025944/http://www.londonwelsh.org/archives/1796 |archive-date=20 July 2011 }}</ref>

==Family==
In 1946 he married Maud Helen Sarah Dashwood,<ref>{{cite web |title=Maud Helen Sarah Bruce (née Dashwood), Lady Aberdare |url=http://www.npg.org.uk/collections/search/person/mp100633/maud-helen-sarah-bruce-nee-dashwood-lady-aberdare |publisher=[[National Portrait Gallery, London]]}}</ref> daughter of [[Sir John Dashwood, 10th Baronet]], and Helen Moira Eaton. They had four children:

* Hon. [[Alastair Bruce, 5th Baron Aberdare|Alastair John Lyndhurst Bruce]] (later 5th Baron; b. 2 May 1947)
* Hon. James Henry Morys Bruce (b. 28 December 1948), married and has issue
* Hon. Henry Adam Francis Bruce (b. 5 February 1962), married and has issue
* Hon. Charles Benjamin Bruce (b. 29 May 1965)

